--TEST--
A live region's rows carry no command byte — every C0, `DEL` and C1 handed to `Core\Cli\Live::set` is substituted exactly as the sink substitutes it, counted over the whole control range — `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink`, `rule:tooling/styling-is-a-value-not-a-grammar` and `rule:tooling/in-place-output-is-a-scoped-live-region`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5's region is the one place a caller most expects a cursor movement to
// pass through: it paints rows over each other, so a `\e[2K` in a row reads
// like part of the drawing rather than like the injection § 1 exists to stop.
// It is not an exception, and this case is what says so — `set` takes
// `array<Cli\Text>` and nothing else, so every row it can be handed came out
// of § 2's constructors with § 1's table already applied to it.
//
// Counted over the whole range rather than read off a line: a table that
// substituted the bytes a test would think to try and passed the rest prints
// plausibly against any single row. § 1 names three classes with different
// answers, so the three counts below are the assertion and the individual
// glyphs are not.
class Sweep {
    // The glyph § 1's table gives `$point`: the U+2400 block is one code point
    // per C0 byte and laid out to match, `DEL` has its own at U+2421, and a C1
    // has no glyph at all, so it becomes U+FFFD.
    public static function glyph(uint $point): string {
        if ($point < 32) {
            return Core\Str::fromCodePoint((9216 as uint) + $point);
        }
        if ($point == 127) {
            return Core\Str::fromCodePoint(9249 as uint);
        }
        return Core\Str::fromCodePoint(65533 as uint);
    }

    // A row is read back with `as string` rather than echoed, so the sink is
    // not in the path at all: `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier` renders a carrier as the bytes it
    // already holds, and those bytes are what `set` was given. A case that
    // echoed the row instead would pass for a `Text` that carried a raw `ESC`,
    // because `echo` would substitute it on the way out.
    public static function over(Core\Cli\Live $live): string {
        int $painted = 0;
        int $rows = 0;
        int $shown = 0;
        int $commands = 0;
        int $layout = 0;
        int $agrees = 0;
        uint $point = 0;
        while ($point < 160) {
            // Everything below the printable range, plus `DEL` and the whole
            // C1 range above it — several terminals still read a C1 as a CSI
            // introducer, which is the row a program cannot have typed by
            // accident and the one an escaper written for `ESC` alone misses.
            if ($point < 32 || $point > 126) {
                string $byte = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($point);
                string $raw = "a" . $byte . "b";
                var $row = Core\Cli\Text::plain($raw);
                $rows = $rows + 1;
                $live->set([$row]);
                $painted = $painted + 1;

                string $back = $row as string;
                // Agreement, over every row: the region, the raw path and
                // `Core\Cli::escape` are one table asked three ways, and a
                // launderer that grew apart from its sink is the failure
                // `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named` exists to prevent.
                if ($back == Core\Cli::escape($raw)) {
                    $agrees = $agrees + 1;
                }
                if ($point == 9 || $point == 10) {
                    // `TAB` and `LF` are layout, not commands: a region draws
                    // rows, so a substitution here would take a program's
                    // ability to lay one out.
                    if (Core\Str::contains($back, $byte)) {
                        $layout = $layout + 1;
                    }
                } else {
                    $commands = $commands + 1;
                    // Both halves: the byte is gone *and* its own glyph is
                    // there. A table that deleted the byte would pass the
                    // first half alone, and § 1 is a substitution.
                    if (!Core\Str::contains($back, $byte)
                        && Core\Str::contains($back, Sweep::glyph($point))) {
                        $shown = $shown + 1;
                    }
                }
            }
            $point = $point + (1 as uint);
        }
        return "a region painted every row " . $painted as string . " of " . $rows as string . "\n"
            . "every command byte shows its own glyph " . $shown as string . " of " . $commands as string . "\n"
            . "layout keeps its own byte " . $layout as string . " of 2" . "\n"
            . "every row is the escaper's own answer " . $agrees as string . " of " . $rows as string;
    }
}

var $counted = Core\Cli::live(fn(Core\Cli\Live $live): string => Sweep::over($live));
echo $counted, "\n";

// The two spellings of the same erase-line command, as a row would carry them.
// Neither is a movement by the time it reaches a region, and the second is the
// one an escaper written against `ESC` would have caught on its own.
var $csi = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(155 as uint);
echo Core\Cli\Text::plain($csi . "2K"), "\n";
echo Core\Cli\Text::plain(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27 as uint) . "[2K"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a region painted every row 65 of 65
every command byte shows its own glyph 63 of 63
layout keeps its own byte 2 of 2
every row is the escaper's own answer 65 of 65
�2K
␛[2K
